WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.468 --> 00:00:08.005
I’m a middle child,
and I was born leaning in.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:09.507
For as long as I can remember,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.778
I've had this insatiable desire
to compete, to accomplish

00:00:13.778 --> 00:00:15.312
and to prove myself.

00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:17.982
My energy isn’t easily contained,

00:00:18.015 --> 00:00:20.651
and I've been called
by both men and women

00:00:20.651 --> 00:00:23.487
intense, high-octane,

00:00:23.487 --> 00:00:25.222
aggressive.

00:00:25.256 --> 00:00:28.092
These words don't feel
like compliments to me.

00:00:29.093 --> 00:00:31.195
I try to control my eagerness.

00:00:31.195 --> 00:00:33.597
I try to be softer.

00:00:33.631 --> 00:00:34.899
It's exhausting.

00:00:34.932 --> 00:00:36.434
(Laughter)

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:40.671
The truth is, this tough shell
conceals sensitivities and insecurities

00:00:40.704 --> 00:00:42.673
that only those closest to me know.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.177
And yeah, my masculine traits
make me the kind of gal

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.978
that can hang with the guys,

00:00:48.012 --> 00:00:49.980
as long as I'm not their boss.

00:00:50.014 --> 00:00:52.216
Last winter, I was walking
through a ski resort

00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:54.185
and a man came up to me and said,

00:00:54.218 --> 00:00:58.489
"Excuse me, are you
with a husband or a fiancée?"

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:01.859
"No," I said, and I continued
toward the lifts.

00:01:01.892 --> 00:01:03.828
And then curiosity got the better of me.

00:01:03.828 --> 00:01:08.132
And I turned around and asked the guy
why he wanted to know if I was with a man.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.603
"I'm selling timeshares.
It's a real estate thing."

00:01:12.636 --> 00:01:14.405
(Laughter)

00:01:16.474 --> 00:01:18.409
"And you don't sell to women?"

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:20.778
"Oh, are you interested?"

00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:22.847
"No."

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.181
(Laughter)

00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:26.517
"But you do know
women carry checkbooks, too?"

00:01:27.318 --> 00:01:31.088
And at that, he looked at me and said,
"Wow, lady, you're tough."

00:01:31.622 --> 00:01:32.890
(Laughter)

00:01:34.658 --> 00:01:37.862
Modern day sexism
is different than it was in the past

00:01:37.862 --> 00:01:40.297
when a blatant comment
about a woman's physique

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:45.503
or a chummy pat on the derriere
was tolerated, maybe even accepted.

00:01:45.536 --> 00:01:48.339
Today's sexism can be more subtle.

00:01:48.372 --> 00:01:53.110
Little nuances that might seem
like no big deal to some,

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.780
but their impact can have
the effect of a thousand cuts.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:02.086
That day at the ski resort resulted
in more than just another microabrasion.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:06.223
It sparked a curiosity in me
about perceptions that we have

00:02:06.257 --> 00:02:08.425
for women versus men.

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.597
I wanted to know why do we perceive women
differently when we assert ourselves.

00:02:13.631 --> 00:02:16.367
So I did a little research
and I came across something,

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.003
just to make sure
this wasn't all on my mind,

00:02:19.036 --> 00:02:21.505
called the competence/likability dilemma,

00:02:21.539 --> 00:02:23.774
where women, unlike men,

00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:27.845
are rarely perceived
to be both competent and likable.

00:02:27.878 --> 00:02:30.614
Now we should take a moment
to define the word "likable."

00:02:30.614 --> 00:02:32.716
Merriam-Webster says "likeable" is:

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.020
"having qualities that bring about
a favorable regard,

00:02:36.053 --> 00:02:38.322
pleasant or agreeable."

00:02:38.322 --> 00:02:40.090
The Urban Dictionary says:

00:02:40.124 --> 00:02:43.594
"likeable, an adjective meaning
pleasant or attractive.

00:02:43.627 --> 00:02:46.630
It describes something
that is easy to like."

00:02:46.664 --> 00:02:52.236
The sad truth is, most of us don't find
strong, competent women easy to like.

00:02:53.671 --> 00:02:56.607
In 2003, Professor Flynn
at Columbia Business School

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.442
conducted an experiment.

00:02:58.442 --> 00:03:00.811
He took a case study about Heidi Roizen,

00:03:00.844 --> 00:03:03.214
a successful female venture capitalist,

00:03:03.247 --> 00:03:05.950
and he changed the name to Howard Roizen.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.320
Everything else about
the case study remained the same.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.656
He gave half of his class
the Heidi Roizen case study

00:03:12.656 --> 00:03:15.693
and the other half
the Howard Roizen case study.

00:03:15.693 --> 00:03:17.795
And he asked the students to rate

00:03:17.828 --> 00:03:22.433
how competent and how likable
Heidi and Howard were.

00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:27.905
Both female and male students found Heidi
and Howard to be equally competent.

00:03:27.905 --> 00:03:30.307
But the students tended not to like Heidi.

00:03:31.041 --> 00:03:35.212
They thought that she was a little
too aggressive and out for herself.

00:03:35.713 --> 00:03:40.651
Neither female nor male students
wanted to work for or hire Heidi,

00:03:40.684 --> 00:03:43.988
but they all thought Howard
would make a great colleague.

00:03:43.988 --> 00:03:45.256
Why?

00:03:45.289 --> 00:03:48.859
Because in our society,
women are penalized

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:52.596
when we behave in ways
that violate gender norms.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.968
Our gender stereotypes show
that women should be kind, nurturing,

00:03:58.002 --> 00:04:01.505
helpful, supportive, deferential,

00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:05.943
while traditionally men
are expected to be decisive, competent,

00:04:05.943 --> 00:04:07.411
assertive and strong.

00:04:08.045 --> 00:04:09.980
So the dilemma for women

00:04:10.014 --> 00:04:12.483
is that the qualities
which we value in leadership,

00:04:12.516 --> 00:04:15.052
such as assertiveness and decisiveness,

00:04:15.085 --> 00:04:18.622
go against societal norms
of what it is to be a likable woman.

00:04:19.423 --> 00:04:23.160
I'm a documentary filmmaker,
so I'm nothing if not curious.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.898
But I've learned that being inquisitive
is not an admired female trait.

00:04:27.931 --> 00:04:29.967
I was at a cocktail party last week,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.670
and I asked a man
what line of business he was in.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.539
“Fintech,” he said.

00:04:36.307 --> 00:04:38.676
Curious. I dug a little deeper.

00:04:38.709 --> 00:04:40.311
"Oh, what type?"

00:04:40.344 --> 00:04:42.313
"It's complicated."

00:04:42.346 --> 00:04:44.014
(Laughter)

00:04:45.449 --> 00:04:46.950
End of discussion.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:51.088
OK. Now it's possible that he wanted
to spare me some long explanation,

00:04:51.121 --> 00:04:55.159
but it's more likely that his bias,
implicit or not,

00:04:55.159 --> 00:04:59.296
informed him that as a woman
I wouldn't understand finance.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:03.734
Either way, I'm fairly certain
he wouldn't have said "it's complicated"

00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:05.436
had I been a man.

00:05:05.469 --> 00:05:07.638
I absorb the sting of one more cut.

00:05:09.473 --> 00:05:14.144
Most of us are not aware of why
we don't find strong women likable.

00:05:14.178 --> 00:05:17.548
There's just something
about her that bugs us.

00:05:17.581 --> 00:05:23.087
But deep inside, at the root of this
is an unconscious bias.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.391
Bias is a survival heuristic
that we inherited from our ancestors.

00:05:27.424 --> 00:05:29.760
The problem is, it's unconscious.

00:05:29.793 --> 00:05:34.631
And as humans, we don't have the ability
to recognize when we're being biased,

00:05:34.665 --> 00:05:37.201
even if we can see it in other people.

00:05:37.201 --> 00:05:41.271
We all, most of us, anyway,
believe ourselves to be fair,

00:05:41.305 --> 00:05:43.574
open-minded and unbiased.

00:05:43.574 --> 00:05:48.379
And yet, I will admit,
I too found Hillary's voice to be shrill.

00:05:48.412 --> 00:05:53.650
As long as society continues to associate
leadership with masculine traits,

00:05:53.684 --> 00:05:56.019
female leaders
will be judged more harshly,

00:05:56.053 --> 00:05:58.489
even when they outperform
male counterparts.

00:05:59.256 --> 00:06:01.358
So it's no wonder
that in the United States,

00:06:01.392 --> 00:06:04.128
female politicians suffer greatly,

00:06:04.161 --> 00:06:08.465
as our elections tend to value
likability over competency.

00:06:09.633 --> 00:06:12.669
Women hold only
25 percent of the seats in Congress.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.607
The competence/likability dilemma
has huge consequences,

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.242
not just in politics,

00:06:18.242 --> 00:06:21.478
but also in education
and in the workplace,

00:06:21.512 --> 00:06:26.150
where studies show that women
in meetings with both genders

00:06:26.183 --> 00:06:28.485
are less likely to contribute knowledge.

00:06:28.485 --> 00:06:30.387
As women we're told to lean in,

00:06:30.421 --> 00:06:32.589
and yet there's backlash when we do.

00:06:33.323 --> 00:06:34.892
A recent study shows that women

00:06:34.925 --> 00:06:38.128
are not as good as men
at negotiating for themselves.

00:06:38.162 --> 00:06:40.798
And yet, women outperform men

00:06:40.831 --> 00:06:43.567
when they negotiate
on behalf of someone else.

00:06:44.535 --> 00:06:47.371
This is because women
who negotiate for themselves

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:50.941
are perceived as selfish,

00:06:50.974 --> 00:06:53.777
and women who negotiate
on behalf of someone else

00:06:53.777 --> 00:06:55.746
are perceived to be helpful.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:02.486
And a helpful woman might be liked more
than a woman who is assertive,

00:07:02.486 --> 00:07:05.055
but the helpful woman
will not be recognized

00:07:05.088 --> 00:07:08.058
as having what it takes
to be a successful leader.

00:07:08.091 --> 00:07:12.696
Last week I was quoting my speaker fee
to a potential client,

00:07:12.696 --> 00:07:15.432
and when I told him the price,
he said to me,

00:07:15.466 --> 00:07:18.168
"Wow. Good for you. That's a lot.

00:07:18.202 --> 00:07:21.905
Isn't documentary filmmaking
kind of, like, your hobby?"

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:23.874
(Laughter)

00:07:24.374 --> 00:07:25.609
Ouch.

00:07:27.678 --> 00:07:31.482
As humans, we're limited
to what we can do to mitigate our biases.

00:07:31.515 --> 00:07:34.852
So even if we were able to,
say, implement a tool

00:07:34.885 --> 00:07:37.721
that would bring more women
into upper management,

00:07:37.754 --> 00:07:42.526
it's highly likely that we would still
evaluate the performance of those women

00:07:42.559 --> 00:07:44.628
with biased perceptions.

00:07:44.661 --> 00:07:48.098
Clearly, leadership and likability
should not be mutually exclusive

00:07:48.131 --> 00:07:49.933
for women or for men.

00:07:49.967 --> 00:07:51.969
So how do we solve for this dilemma?

00:07:52.736 --> 00:07:56.406
I wish I could offer you
a cure to unconscious bias.

00:07:56.406 --> 00:07:57.841
I don't believe there is one.

00:07:58.842 --> 00:08:01.378
But we need to disrupt stereotypes

00:08:01.411 --> 00:08:05.182
and redefine what it is
to be a leader and a woman.

00:08:05.215 --> 00:08:08.719
Obviously, this is a long-term solution
that could take generations,

00:08:08.752 --> 00:08:10.487
but it's a vital step.

00:08:10.487 --> 00:08:12.256
We need to speak up.

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:14.992
Speak up when you witness gender bias.

00:08:14.992 --> 00:08:16.527
Question comments that are made

00:08:16.527 --> 00:08:21.265
about a woman's appearance or personality
rather than her skills or ability.

00:08:22.633 --> 00:08:25.002
And we need to slow down.

00:08:26.703 --> 00:08:31.508
Question yourself.
Reconsider your snap judgments.

00:08:31.542 --> 00:08:36.146
Ask yourself, does that woman bug me
for reasons that really matter?

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:38.382
And forget about likability,

00:08:38.415 --> 00:08:40.284
ladies, women, if you can.

00:08:41.318 --> 00:08:43.987
Instead focus on being awesome.

00:08:43.987 --> 00:08:46.757
Be a smart, compassionate,
effective leader

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:50.961
that will redefine the stereotype
of what a female leader is.

00:08:50.994 --> 00:08:54.097
I've wasted a lot of time
worrying about how I am perceived.

00:08:55.098 --> 00:08:58.835
If I ask a lot of questions,
will you find me combative?

00:08:59.736 --> 00:09:02.673
If I tell you about
an accomplishment I'm proud of,

00:09:02.706 --> 00:09:04.374
you might think I'm conceited.

00:09:06.176 --> 00:09:11.448
But I need and seek
the approval and recognition

00:09:11.448 --> 00:09:15.485
of my family, of my friends
and of my colleagues.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:19.423
Like many of you, I want to be liked,

00:09:19.456 --> 00:09:22.159
and I'm hopeful for the day
when women can be recognized

00:09:22.159 --> 00:09:25.429
as being competent and likable,

00:09:25.462 --> 00:09:28.765
a day when we value each other
regardless of gender,

00:09:28.799 --> 00:09:31.802
for the unique contributions
that we each bring to the table.

00:09:32.736 --> 00:09:36.340
That would be the day that
I won't have to worry about being liked.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:38.308
I can just be me.

00:09:40.077 --> 00:09:41.111
Thank you.

00:09:41.144 --> 00:09:44.848
(Applause)

